<http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/PH> a ns1:Unit ;
    rdfs:label "Acidity"@en ;
    dcterms:description """\r
  In chemistry the unit $\\textit{pH}$, also referred to as $\\textit{acidity}$ or $\\textit{basicity}$,\r
   is the negative logarithm (base 10) of the concentration of free protons (or hydronium ions).\r
  The definition of $pH$ in terms of hydrogen ions in solution is:\r
\r
  $$\\text{pH}=-\\log_{10}(a_{H^+})\\equiv-\\log_{10}\\left(\\left[H^+\\right]\\right)$$\r
\r
  Where $a_{H^+}$ is the equilibrium molar concentration of $H^+$ in the solution, the activity of\r
   the hydrogen ion in the solution.\r
\r
  This definition is appropriate for concentrations equal to, or less than $1\\ mol/l$, \r
   where $aH+ \\equiv [H+]$, that is, $1\\ mol/L\\ HCl$ has a $pH$ of zero.\r
  \r
  To relate this to standard molality ($b^\\circ$), consideration is given to the activity ($a_{H^+}$)\r
   of the hydrogen ions is the product of their molality ($m_{H^+}$), and the activity coefficient ($\\gamma_{H^+}$),\r
   where $m_{H^+}$ is expressed in $mol/kg$, and $b^\\circ$ is typically taken as $1 \\ mol/kg$.\r
  \r
  \r
  The activity can then be expressed as:\r
\r
  $$a_{H^+} = \\gamma_{H^+} \\times m_{H^+}$$ \r
\r
  Where, $\\gamma_{H^+}$ is the activity coefficient, which adjusts the molality to account for\r
   non-ideal behavior due to interactions between ions in the solution.\r
\r
  The expansion of $pH$ then becomes:\r
\r
  $$\\text{pH} = -log_{10}\\left(m_{H+}\\times\\gamma_{H^+}\\right)$$\r
\r
  Where, $m_{H+}$ is the molality of hydrogen ions in the solution relative to the standard \r
    molality, which can also be expressed as $b^{\\circ}$.\r
\r
This definition is relevant in more concentrated solutions or when precise thermodynamic\r
 calculations are required. \r
It reflects how the properties of the solution deviate from ideal behavior and provides a more accurate\r
 understanding of the $pH$ under various conditions.\r
\r
While $pH$ is a universally recognized scale for expressing hydrogen ion activity,\r
  its appropriateness and accuracy can diminish under conditions of extremely high\r
  ionic strength, non-aqueous environments, high temperatures, or very high or\r
  low $pH$ values.\r
In such cases, alternative measurement strategies might be required to obtain\r
   meaningful and accurate descriptions of acidity or basicity.\r
"""^^ns1:LatexString ;
    ns1:applicableSystem <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/ASU>,
        <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/CGS>,
        <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/CGS-EMU>,
        <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/CGS-ESU>,
        <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/CGS-GAUSS>,
        <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/IMPERIAL>,
        <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/PLANCK>,
        <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/SI> ;
    ns1:hasDimensionVector <http://qudt.org/vocab/dimensionvector/A0E0L0I0M0H0T0D1> ;
    ns1:hasQuantityKind <http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind/Acidity>,
        <http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind/Basicity> ;
    ns1:informativeReference "https://iupac.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/05/IUPAC-GB3-2012-2ndPrinting-PDFsearchable.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    ns1:symbol "pH" ;
    ns1:ucumCode "[pH]"^^ns1:UCUMcs ;
    rdfs:comment "Unsure about dimensionality of pH; conversion requires a log function not just a multiplier"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/vocab/unit> .

